          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Niels Bohr's Early Life</strong><br>\nNiels Bohr was born in Copenhagen, Denmark, in 1885. From a young age, he showed a keen interest in science, influenced by his father, who was a professor of physiology at the University of Copenhagen. Bohr excelled in his academic pursuits and went on to study physics at the same university. His early work focused on the structure of atoms and led him to develop a new model of atomic structure in 1913.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Bohr's Atomic Model</strong><br>\nBohr proposed a new model of the atom that introduced the concept of energy levels. According to his model, electrons orbit the nucleus in fixed paths, or shells, but can jump from one level or orbit to another. This groundbreaking idea helped explain how atoms emit and absorb energy, which was a significant step forward in the development of quantum mechanics.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Contribution to Quantum Theory</strong><br>\nNiels Bohr's contributions to quantum theory were profound. He worked on the principle of complementarity, which posits that items can be separately analyzed as having several contradictory properties. For instance, light can be described both as a wave and as a particle. His interpretation played a crucial role in understanding atomic and molecular structure.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
