            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Nobel Prize in Physics</strong><br>\nIn recognition of his contributions to our understanding of atomic structure and quantum mechanics, Niels Bohr was awarded the Nobel Prize in Physics in 1922. His work not only laid the foundation for atomic research but also for the study of quantum phenomena in complex systems.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Bohr Institute of Theoretical Physics</strong><br>\nIn 1920, Bohr founded the Institute of Theoretical Physics at the University of Copenhagen, which became a center for scientists working on the theoretical aspects of physics. The institute attracted many prominent scientists and played a key role in the development of modern physics.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Bohr and the Manhattan Project</strong><br>\nDuring World War II, Bohr was involved in the Allied atomic bomb project, known as the Manhattan Project. He contributed to solving the problem of nuclear fission and shared his concerns about the consequences of nuclear warfare with leaders such as President Franklin Roosevelt and Prime Minister Winston Churchill.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
