          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Post-War Efforts and Open World</strong><br>\nAfter the war, Bohr became an advocate for the peaceful application of atomic energy and international cooperation. He promoted the idea of an \"open world\" where nations would share knowledge and work together for the betterment of humanity, emphasizing the importance of scientific exchange and diplomacy.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Philosophy and Scientific Methodology</strong><br>\nBohr was also known for his philosophical approach to science. He believed that science progresses through the interplay of experiments and theory. This perspective led him to engage in debates on scientific methodology and the philosophy of science, particularly on the topics of objectivity and observation.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy and Influence</strong><br>\nNiels Bohr passed away in 1962, but his legacy continues to influence scientific thought and research. The concepts he introduced have become fundamental to our understanding of the physical world, and his name is honored in various ways, including the element named bohrium, after him. He left behind a rich scientific heritage that continues to inspire physicists around the world.</p>\n"
              },
